Remember the file name of a successful r, not of a failed one.
The test after readLines was inverted: a successful r left the
remembered file name unset, while an r that failed set it to the file
that could not be read, so a later w with no name wrote the buffer to
that name. Set the name, when there is none yet, only after a
successful read, as POSIX describes.

---
 editors/ed.c |    4 +---
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 3 deletions(-)

--- a/editors/ed.c
+++ b/editors/ed.c
@@ -945,9 +945,7 @@
 			}
 			if (!have1)
 				num1 = lastNum;
-			if (readLines(cp, num1 + 1))
-				break;
-			if (fileName == NULL)
+			if (readLines(cp, num1 + 1) && fileName == NULL)
 				fileName = xstrdup(cp);
 			break;
 
